---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
const svc = `osewa service install                 # start now and at every login or boot
osewa service install --listen :9000  # the same, on another port
osewa service uninstall               # stop it and remove the unit`;
const daemon = `$ osewa server --daemon
osewa server: started (pid 4821, [::]:7322, log /home/me/.local/state/osewa/server.log)`;
const port = `# on the host
osewa service install --listen :9000

# on your machine
osewa login --service --addr devbox.example.com:9000 me@devbox`;
const update = `osewa update devbox
copied osewa (linux/amd64) to devbox
devbox's server restarted with it`;
---
<Docs slug="server" description="Run the osewa server as a systemd or launchd user service, change its port, open the firewall, update it, and find its log.">
<p class="lede">Each user on a host runs their own osewa server, as that user. It listens on one port, 7322 by default, and starts a process per session. When another user's server already has 7322, login gives this user's the next free port (7323 and up), keeps it in <code>~/.config/osewa/port</code> on the host, and records it on your machine. <code>osewa login --service</code> sets all of this up; this page is for changing it.</p>

<h2>As a service</h2>
<pre><code>{svc}</code></pre>
<p>A service restarts the server if it crashes and starts it again after a reboot.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Linux</th><th>macOS</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Manager</td><td>systemd, user unit</td><td>launchd, user agent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>File</td><td><code>~/.config/systemd/user/osewa.service</code></td><td><code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.osewa.server.plist</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Status</td><td><code>systemctl --user status osewa</code></td><td><code>launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/dev.osewa.server</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Linux: enable lingering.</strong> systemd stops a user's services when they log out, unless lingering is on. On a server you reach only over ssh, run this once:</p>
<pre style="margin: 8px 0 0"><code>loginctl enable-linger $USER</code></pre></div>
<p>Running <code>service install</code> again rewrites the unit with the new flags and restarts the server. A server started with <code>--daemon</code> is stopped first.</p>

<h2>Without a service manager</h2>
<p><code>osewa server --daemon</code> detaches into the background, waits until the server answers, and reports:</p>
<pre><code>{daemon}</code></pre>
<p>It won't come back after a reboot. If a server is already running, it says so and leaves it alone. Plain <code>osewa server</code> runs in the foreground, logging to the terminal.</p>
<p>Only one server runs per user. A second one fails with <code>osewa server: already running (pid N)</code>.</p>

<h2 id="flags">Server flags</h2>
<p>These go after <code>osewa server</code> or <code>osewa service install</code>. There is no config file.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--listen :7322</code></td><td>The address and port. TCP and UDP use the same number. <code>:PORT</code> listens on every interface, IPv6 and IPv4.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--name N</code></td><td>The name the server gives itself. Defaults to the hostname.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-quic</code></td><td>Don't listen on UDP; clients use the TLS WebSocket only.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-compress</code></td><td>Don't compress what goes to clients over the WebSocket.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-forward</code></td><td>Refuse port forwards.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-exec</code></td><td>Refuse commands without a terminal. This also stops <code>osewa update</code> from working.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="port">Changing the port</h2>
<p>login records the port the host's enroll reports, the one in <code>~/.config/osewa/port</code> or 7322, not one given with <code>--listen</code>. So after moving the server with <code>--listen</code>, log in again with <code>--addr</code> to point your machine at the new port:</p>
<pre><code>{port}</code></pre>
<p>Sessions survive the move: they don't live in the server. Do the second step on every device that uses the host.</p>
<p>If 7322 is blocked where you are and only common ports get through, binding 443 needs root. Instead, keep osewa on a high port and redirect 443 to it with the host's firewall.</p>

<h2>Firewalls</h2>
<p>Open the port for TCP and UDP. With only TCP open, everything works over the TLS WebSocket, but each connection takes about 300 ms longer while QUIC is tried first, and you lose QUIC's separate streams. If the UDP port can't be bound, the server logs <code>osewa server: no QUIC: listen udp ...</code> and serves TCP alone.</p>

<h2 id="updating">Updating</h2>
<p>Update a host from your machine, over osewa, without ssh:</p>
<pre><code>{update}</code></pre>
<p>It copies your build of osewa to the host's <code>~/.local/bin/osewa</code>, checks its hash, and restarts the server with it. Sessions keep running across the restart, and your attached windows reconnect. If the new build has a different protocol or snapshot version, older sessions keep running but can't be attached any more; see <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions</a>. The host gets the build for its own platform, found the same way as at <a href="/docs/install">login</a>.</p>
<div class="note"><p>A server started with <code>--daemon</code> comes back from an update with default flags: a custom <code>--listen</code> is lost. Run it as a service to keep its flags.</p></div>
<p>Your machine and the host must run compatible builds: the same protocol version and the same snapshot version (they may link different libghostty-vt commits). When they don't, commands fail with <code>update osewa on the host or here</code>.</p>

<h3 id="auto-update">Auto-update</h3>
<p>By default a released osewa checks the <a href="https://github.com/rjyo/osewa/releases">GitHub releases</a> and tells you when there's a newer one, but runs nothing new until you do: an update runs what it downloads, and osewa has access to your machines.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>On your machine</strong>, after a command run in a terminal, osewa checks at most once a day, in the background. Later commands say when a release is out; <code>osewa update</code> installs it.</li>
  <li><strong>A server</strong> run as a service or with <code>--daemon</code> checks every hour and says so in its log. Update it from your machine with <code>osewa update &lt;host&gt;</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>osewa set auto-update auto</code> installs releases by itself instead:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>On your machine</strong>, a newer release that works with this one is installed over it; you get it from the next command.</li>
  <li><strong>A server</strong> installs a newer release that works with this one, waits until no port forward or command is running (a restart would end them; sessions survive it), and restarts into it. Attached windows reconnect.</li>
  <li><strong>A release that can't talk to this one</strong> (another protocol or snapshot version) is still never installed by itself: it would leave every running session unattachable and lock out devices still on this build. osewa says it's out, after commands and in the server log. Update everything together when you're ready: <code>osewa update</code> here, then <code>osewa update &lt;host&gt;</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>osewa set auto-update off</code> stops checking. A host's setting is its own: run it there. <code>OSEWA_AUTO_UPDATE=off</code> in the environment overrides the setting. Builds from source never check on their own.</p>

<h2>Logs</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Started as</th><th>Log</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Linux service</td><td>the journal: <code>journalctl --user -u osewa</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>macOS service</td><td><code>~/.local/state/osewa/server.log</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--daemon</code></td><td><code>~/.local/state/osewa/server.log</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>foreground</td><td>the terminal</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p><code>$XDG_STATE_HOME</code>, when set, replaces <code>~/.local/state</code>.</p>
</Docs>
